From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] icuuc: the system ICU's character set converters (ucnv_*)

Windows 10 and later carry ICU in system32 and programs built against the system ICU import icuuc.dll by its
unversioned names; Wine had no icuuc.dll, so KeePassXC 2.8 (its Qt6Core imports icuuc's converters for Qt's text
codecs) did not start (c0000135). This is our own implementation of the documented ICU converter API:
ucnv_open/close/reset, names (ICU's loose name matching, aliases, getName, getStandardName for MIME and IANA,
countAvailable/getAvailableName), getMaxCharSize, streaming toUnicode/fromUnicode with incomplete characters held
back between calls, output that does not fit kept for the next call, offsets, the error callbacks (the documented
SUBSTITUTE ones, a program's own, ucnv_cbToUWriteUChars/ucnv_cbFromUWriteUChars, RESET and CLOSE), and the pending
counts. UTF-8/16/32 are converted directly (UTF-16/32 read a byte order mark and write one, then the platform's
order, as ICU does); other charsets go through the system's code pages one character at a time so each bad
sequence reaches the callback where it is. Only icuuc.dll: programs that want all of ICU (.NET looks for icu.dll,
then icuuc.dll with icuin.dll) still do not find it and keep using NLS.
---
